      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "Label": "Objective",
          "content": "Physicians should be aware of patients trying to obtain a diagnosis for secondary gain. Malingering is a diagnosis that should be suspected when objective findings do not support the subjective symptoms and there is secondary gain."
        },
        {
          "Label": "Methods",
          "content": "A series of 21 cases are presented that support this position. The charts of 21 patients with a diagnosis of reflex sympathetic dystrophy (chronic regional pain syndrome) and nonanatomic findings were evaluated."
        },
        {
          "Label": "Results",
          "content": "The patients in this series were found to be malingering based on discrepancies between subjective symptoms and objective findings."
        },
        {
          "Label": "Conclusions",
          "content": "The diagnosis of malingering should be based on thorough history, physical examination, electrodiagnostic studies, imaging studies, and evaluation of all medical records."
        }
      ]},
